From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 19:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] server: on PS5, a snapshot of every thread's wait for a game that does not end

With the in-process synchronization device on (patch 0890, WINE_PS5_NTSYNC=1), a .NET game's close froze
in four runs of six: after WM_CLOSE no frame, no server request, and only the audio mixer (which
needs neither the server nor the device) kept running. Without the device it never froze. To see
where the threads are, the title asks for a snapshot when a game has not ended 10 s after its close:

- pw_wineserver_snapshot (exported through ntdll as pw_wine_server_snapshot) prints the wait
  watchdog's snapshot (patch 0680) once, from the calling thread under the server lock. Each thread's
  line now carries its kernel thread id and the device number of its own sync object, so the
  device's dump of blocked waits can be read against it. A lock that is not free within the given
  time is reported with the kernel thread that holds it and how often it was taken meanwhile.
- The server records which kernel thread holds its lock, at every place the lock is taken or
  released (the main loop and client threads running requests, patch 0918).
- get_inproc_sync_fd tells the device what a number stands for (the object's type, and the thread or
  process id, else the handle), through a new note operation in ps5_ntsync_ops.
- The watchdog's guest-stack lines read the WoW64 CPU area only in a WoW64 process: a 64-bit
  process keeps its own data in that TLS slot, and a thread blocked in the device is silent long
  enough to reach that code.
